1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,911
There is a version of your voice that everyone you know has heard.

2
00:00:04,331 --> 00:00:06,944
Your friends. Your family. Your teachers.

3
00:00:07,364 --> 00:00:09,059
Everyone... except you.

4
00:00:09,479 --> 00:00:14,023
So why do so many of us hate the sound of our own recorded voice?

5
00:00:14,443 --> 00:00:19,120
It usually starts with something simple. Like sending a voice message.

6
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:21,683
[Stickman] Hey! It's me. Call me back when you get this!

7
00:00:22,103 --> 00:00:25,118
Then, just out of curiosity, you press play.

8
00:00:25,538 --> 00:00:27,682
[Stickman] Hey! It's me. Call me back when you get this!

9
00:00:28,102 --> 00:00:29,769
[Stickman] Wait... is that what I sound like?

10
00:00:30,189 --> 00:00:37,046
If that made you cringe, you're not alone. Lots of people can't stand hearing their own recorded voice.

11
00:00:37,466 --> 00:00:41,548
But here's the strange part. The recording isn't lying to you.

12
00:00:41,968 --> 00:00:45,495
It's actually much closer to how everyone else hears you.

13
00:00:45,915 --> 00:00:50,468
To understand why, let's look at what happens every time you speak.

14
00:00:50,888 --> 00:00:53,599
When you talk, your vocal cords vibrate.

15
00:00:54,019 --> 00:01:00,152
Those vibrations push sound waves out of your mouth, through the air, and into your ears.

16
00:01:00,572 --> 00:01:04,758
That's the first road. And it's exactly how other people hear you.

17
00:01:05,178 --> 00:01:08,546
But there's a second road. One that only you have.

18
00:01:08,966 --> 00:01:16,310
The same vibrations travel through your throat, your jaw, and the bones of your skull, straight to your inner ear.

19
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:19,163
Scientists call this bone conduction.

20
00:01:19,583 --> 00:01:23,605
And your skull is especially good at carrying the low, deep notes.

21
00:01:24,025 --> 00:01:29,751
So the voice you hear in your head is a mix of both roads. Deeper. Fuller. Richer.

22
00:01:30,171 --> 00:01:33,691
It's the voice you've heard every single day of your life.

23
00:01:34,111 --> 00:01:37,625
A microphone, though, only catches the first road.

24
00:01:38,045 --> 00:01:41,046
It can't hear the vibrations inside your skull.

25
00:01:41,466 --> 00:01:44,832
So when you play it back, those deep notes are gone.

26
00:01:45,252 --> 00:01:49,195
What's left sounds thinner, higher, and strangely unfamiliar.

27
00:01:49,615 --> 00:01:52,657
Like a stranger doing a bad impression of you.

28
00:01:53,077 --> 00:01:58,532
Here's a rough idea of the difference. First, something like the voice in your head.

29
00:01:58,952 --> 00:02:00,364
[Stickman] This is how I sound to me.

30
00:02:00,784 --> 00:02:02,321
And now, the recording.

31
00:02:02,741 --> 00:02:04,538
[Stickman] This is how I sound to everyone else.

32
00:02:05,058 --> 00:02:09,777
You can test this right now. Put your fingers in your ears and say something.

33
00:02:10,197 --> 00:02:12,160
Go on. Nobody's watching.

34
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:20,403
Hear how deep and boomy it sounds? Blocking your ears cuts out most of the air road, so you mostly hear the bone road.

35
00:02:20,823 --> 00:02:23,832
That's the hidden bass you never knew was there.

36
00:02:24,252 --> 00:02:32,463
But bone conduction only explains why a recording sounds different. It doesn't fully explain why it makes us cringe.

37
00:02:32,883 --> 00:02:40,909
In 1966, two psychologists, Philip Holzman and Clyde Rousey, studied this exact feeling.

38
00:02:41,329 --> 00:02:43,482
They called it voice confrontation.

39
00:02:43,902 --> 00:02:49,215
When people heard someone else's recording, they paid attention to what was being said.

40
00:02:49,635 --> 00:02:55,002
But when they heard their own voice, they focused on how it sounded. And they didn't like it.

41
00:02:55,422 --> 00:03:06,766
The researchers suggested that recordings reveal things we don't notice while we're talking. A nervous tone. A hesitation. Little clues about how we come across.

42
00:03:07,186 --> 00:03:11,098
Things that don't match the confident voice we hear in our heads.

43
00:03:11,518 --> 00:03:13,960
Something similar happens with photos.

44
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:24,528
In a 1977 experiment, researchers showed a group of students two photos of their own face: a normal one, and a mirror-flipped one.

45
00:03:24,948 --> 00:03:32,950
Most preferred the flipped one: the face they see in the mirror every day. But their close friends preferred the normal photo.

46
00:03:33,370 --> 00:03:36,104
Each side liked the version they were used to.

47
00:03:36,524 --> 00:03:43,936
Psychologists call this the mere exposure effect. The more familiar something is, the more we tend to like it.

48
00:03:44,356 --> 00:03:50,913
And your voice works the same way. You're used to the version in your head, so the real one sounds wrong.

49
00:03:51,333 --> 00:03:52,852
But here's the twist.

50
00:03:53,272 --> 00:04:03,773
In 2013, researchers asked eighty people to rate how attractive a set of recorded voices sounded. Their own voice was secretly mixed in.

51
00:04:04,193 --> 00:04:09,744
The result? People rated their own voice as more attractive than other listeners did.

52
00:04:10,164 --> 00:04:13,151
And more attractive than everyone else's voice.

53
00:04:13,571 --> 00:04:18,633
So maybe the problem isn't your voice at all. Maybe it's knowing that it's yours.

54
00:04:19,053 --> 00:04:28,223
The good news? The cringe fades. Radio hosts, singers, YouTubers... people who hear their own voice every day often get used to it.

55
00:04:28,643 --> 00:04:31,714
The more you hear it, the more normal it sounds.

56
00:04:32,134 --> 00:04:40,863
So next time you hear a recording of yourself, remember: that voice isn't worse. It's just the one you've never heard from the outside.

57
00:04:41,283 --> 00:04:44,397
It's the voice your friends recognize instantly.

58
00:04:44,817 --> 00:04:45,545
[Stickman] Hey!

59
00:04:45,605 --> 00:04:49,730
That's the voice that makes someone turn around when you call their name.

60
00:04:50,150 --> 00:04:53,230
Everyone in your life already knows that voice.

61
00:04:53,650 --> 00:04:56,001
Maybe it's time you got to know it too.

62
00:04:56,421 --> 00:05:00,270
So... do you hate your recorded voice? Tell me in the comments.

63
00:05:00,690 --> 00:05:04,176
And follow for more strange questions about your brain.
